WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:14.718
- [Interviewer] All right, Evelyn,

00:00:14.718 --> 00:00:15.551
- [Evelyn] Yes?

00:00:15.551 --> 00:00:16.460
- [Interviewer] Evelyn,
where'd you grow up?

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:17.925
Where are you from originally?

00:00:17.925 --> 00:00:19.910
- [Evelyn] I'm from a river town called

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:21.333
Richland, Washington.

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:24.390
- [Interviewer] Is that near Seattle?

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:27.650
- It's downwind from Seattle,

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:29.260
near Hanford, Washington,

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:32.193
where metal cars and
refrigerators are made.

00:00:33.218 --> 00:00:34.051
- [Interviewer] And tell
me about your childhood,

00:00:34.051 --> 00:00:35.090
you had both your parents?

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:36.160
- Yes, I did.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:40.929
I had an elderly father
and a brunette mother.

00:00:40.929 --> 00:00:43.040
- [Interviewer] How, how
was, how was your childhood?

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:44.990
- It was fine, lots of running

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:49.990
and early dance lessons
with the Russian masters.

00:00:51.025 --> 00:00:53.000
- [Interviewer] What kind of
kid were you in high school?

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.350
- I was an early listener.

00:00:56.350 --> 00:01:01.080
I would come in at about
two o'clock in the afternoon

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:04.963
not wanting to be in sweated
clothes or slip on the desk.

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:09.760
And I would put my feet
up on the desk,

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:11.343
and not look around.

00:01:12.919 --> 00:01:16.333
And, the first word was Paris Review.

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:22.650
And we were able to travel
the ski lodges that summer,

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:25.683
and see the mountains
as they were iced up.

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:31.240
And we just wore short sleeve t-shirts

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:34.520
and our exposure and teenage years,

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:37.470
and went to the desert after that

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:42.470
for some nude running, and
found myself near a shop.

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:45.670
Returned home with a
dress and found myself

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:50.140
in a long bugety dress and
hung it out on the line.

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:51.290
And the woman appeared,

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:53.483
and she grew to be my sister, Jean.

00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:58.290
And she taught me sewing,

00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:02.373
It was from a black enamel
S inger sewing machine,

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.150
and it kept the house cool.

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:10.140
And we poured fudge that
Christmas and my brother appeared.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:13.483
And he's now a minister at
Glide Memorial hospital.

00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:16.440
- [Interviewer] So your
childhood sounds pretty good.

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:19.220
- Yeah, not a lot of time with my parents,

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:22.780
as I had to make my own
money for my future college.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:26.940
I had a set on my heart as a
youngster to remain with books.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:29.690
even if my mother and
father went to the parks

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:32.440
or accident or injury.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:37.330
And my elder sister took me
into her home for three years.

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:41.280
As I watched her growing
through her early marriage.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:46.093
Now she's been married
up for past 55 years.

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:48.460
- [Interviewer] How old are you Evelyn? 

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:50.390
- I'm 61.

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:51.641
- [Interviewer] Have you been married?

00:02:51.641 --> 00:02:53.182
- No I haven't.

00:02:53.182 --> 00:02:54.090
- [Interviewer] You have children.

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:55.723
- Yes, I do. I have 2.

00:02:55.723 --> 00:02:57.010
- [Interviewer] And did you raise them?

00:02:57.010 --> 00:03:01.250
- Yes. Yes I did, along with my sisters.

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:04.960
And my three brothers also have children,

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:09.340
and he played and they
sorted the toys out.

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:11.059
- [Interviewer] So today you're
living in the Tenderloin,

00:03:11.059 --> 00:03:11.892
- Yeah.

00:03:11.892 --> 00:03:12.725
- [Interviewer] San Francisco.

00:03:12.725 --> 00:03:13.558
- Yeah.

00:03:13.558 --> 00:03:15.030
- [Interviewer] How long
have you been living here?

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:20.030
- Since the year 2000, stopping
in front of the pop shop

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:22.613
and getting cherry Cola.

00:03:23.861 --> 00:03:27.944
♪ Cherry cola, ch-ch-ch-ch-Cola. ♪

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:31.900
Yeah and that brought the train to stop,

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:34.423
as it nearly went off the road.

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:38.440
And I was able to hang
from the dung sport.

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:42.193
Those old mule lines and clip those.

00:03:43.047 --> 00:03:44.013
- [Interviewer] Are you
living on the streets now?

00:03:44.013 --> 00:03:45.633
- with a squiggle straw.

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:50.780
Yeah, mostly to keep the roof up, as I'm a weightlifter

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:55.203
in my New York years running
track and shooting baskets.

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:57.810
And then that brought me home

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:00.670
to my father's old globe head.

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:03.820
And he didn't take me in,

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:06.290
He told me the sky is falling,

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:07.823
and I went in the back,

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:10.540
and found the typewriter.

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:15.540
He took electrical engineering
from about the year 1919.

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:20.360
and I was able to write a full screenplay,

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:22.083
called The Sky is Blue.

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:26.450
And took it to New York and deposited it

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:29.090
in the glove in the bin,

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:30.623
the Franklin mint.

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:34.863
And it came up as diamond earrings,

00:04:37.597 --> 00:04:39.290
and elevator.

00:04:39.290 --> 00:04:42.770
And I was in that elevator
rising to NBC studios

00:04:42.770 --> 00:04:44.283
where I met Donald Trump.

00:04:45.569 --> 00:04:47.560
- [Interviewer] Do you
live in a tent today?

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:49.660
- I'd been in two tents,

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:53.593
did not enjoy the "squoshen" conditions.

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:56.230
- [Interviewer] So you're
basically just living

00:04:56.230 --> 00:04:57.410
on the street now without a tent.

00:04:57.410 --> 00:05:00.310
- Yeah. There's a little

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:03.356
zipper job there,

00:05:03.356 --> 00:05:05.600
and those singer black hat.

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:06.433
- [Interviewer] What
do you do for a living?

00:05:06.433 --> 00:05:07.350
- The tent got too hot.

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:09.920
So I said, Keith Sweat,

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:13.783
and did like the black guys
do and paddled out on my own.

00:05:15.199 --> 00:05:16.170
- [Interviewer] Do you have
rough things happen to you

00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:17.003
here on the streets?

00:05:17.003 --> 00:05:17.970
- Rough things, no.

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:21.510
The worst I've had, is a cigarette burn

00:05:21.510 --> 00:05:24.912
to my inner thigh, and that was looking

00:05:24.912 --> 00:05:27.940
at the boys' hand who
kept a pipe in his hands

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:32.020
hearing arrest from citizen officers.

00:05:32.020 --> 00:05:34.160
They can be dressed in day clothes, or

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:37.350
as you'll find pizza dinner

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:40.030
followed by root beer and Coke.

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:41.950
And that will make an officer,

00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:44.230
if you've in need of a parent,

00:05:44.230 --> 00:05:45.733
but if you have your parents,

00:05:46.623 --> 00:05:48.730
then they can speak to them for you

00:05:48.730 --> 00:05:51.160
if you're dragged
through the court system.

00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:52.620
- [Interviewer] Evelyn, do
you use any of the drugs

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:53.810
that are on the street?

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:58.810
- I used marijuana from a thing
like McDonald's wrappers.

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:02.990
Those are less, you can leave
the chunk of beef in there

00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:05.883
and you'll later find
your marijuana cigarette.

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:10.043
- [Interviewer] Have you ever,

00:06:10.043 --> 00:06:12.440
you ever been in a mental health facility

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:13.273
or anything?

00:06:13.273 --> 00:06:15.420
- Yes, I went voluntarily,

00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:18.283
to get the temperature of my outdoor body.

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:21.820
And was able to walk out with black shoes

00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:24.323
and take a bus ride to a Meineke shop,

00:06:25.190 --> 00:06:29.130
where I saw the Japanese hostesses,

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:32.923
with there Sherbet hair
that is running sweat.

00:06:33.910 --> 00:06:37.760
As their toddlers were
curbed to the street

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:40.270
and netted behind their backs,

00:06:40.270 --> 00:06:43.100
probably nauseous and overheated.

00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:45.270
Maybe an infant mortality.

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:47.533
And I went back out to the hospital,

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:53.400
to get thermometers and
band-aids and found them just

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:57.000
outside the door and was
lucky to get away again.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:01.640
Cause mental institutions
are mostly MC Hammer

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:06.000
or old cereal scoops,
those little Cheerios.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:07.823
Those will make a nurse in a hurry.

00:07:09.330 --> 00:07:10.450
And that's on the floor.

00:07:10.450 --> 00:07:13.840
There'll just be a double bed.

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:16.217
- [Interviewer] Does your
family know that you're done?

00:07:16.217 --> 00:07:17.150
Does your family know
that you're living here

00:07:17.150 --> 00:07:17.983
on the streets?

00:07:17.983 --> 00:07:18.942
- Oh yes.

00:07:18.942 --> 00:07:20.380
I wrote them for about 12 years,

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:22.410
an easy dozen,

00:07:22.410 --> 00:07:25.380
pieces of paper were rolled up

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:28.750
and made into smoking papers.

00:07:28.750 --> 00:07:31.783
Which I deposited into a,

00:07:33.890 --> 00:07:36.970
a rake for the parks.

00:07:36.970 --> 00:07:40.510
And that made my uncle come up

00:07:40.510 --> 00:07:44.160
from Milwaukee, an old beer drinking town.

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:47.310
Where the Buffalo would
come to the back door

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:50.240
and Grace, the nephew of the bartender.

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:54.193
And he'll look like a
grateful dead member,

00:07:55.250 --> 00:07:57.500
with their guitar strung around the necks

00:07:57.500 --> 00:07:59.850
and sweating up in the bugs,

00:07:59.850 --> 00:08:04.140
coming from the laces of the, the cords.

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:06.300
Those are all keys that had been

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:08.513
strewn around the California pool.

00:08:09.430 --> 00:08:11.207
- [Interviewer] Evelyn, how do you?

00:08:11.207 --> 00:08:12.750
- [Evelyn] How do I spell my name?

00:08:12.750 --> 00:08:15.490
- [Interviewer] No. How do you,
how do you support yourself?

00:08:15.490 --> 00:08:16.912
